@0    = ~Who are--ah, you must be the one who cleansed the Visionhive. I greet you, <PRO_RACE>. Forgive me my confusion, I'm still feeling somewhat numb. It's been a while since I've walked or done anything else.~
@1    = ~Numb? You're a spectre, aren't you?~
@2    = ~I had no idea there was someone else in here.~
@3    = ~Agh... another spirit. Great...~
@4    = ~It's because your form needs some time to stabilize, ghost.~
@5    = ~Yes. Not physically, of course. It's simply my form. I have rested for years and need to stabilize. So many years have passed since I walked as a living man... but I'll be fine in a moment.~
@6    = ~Well, it's the Visionhive. There's almost always someone here, though not always materially. There are many spirits in these walls and roots. They are Visionhive's lifeblood.~
@7    = ~And what else did you expect? This is the Visionhive - a home to many spirits and ghosts. They are the lifeblood of these roots and walls. They are the many in one, and one - the Visionhive - is them all.~
@8    = ~You're a shaman too, then. You know the rules. I am honoured to meet one of my own.~
@9    = ~My name is Will. Will Rattlemoth. A shaman and one of the spirits of the Visionhive.~
@10   = ~Will? Quite an usual name for someone like you. Erm... don't take it personally, I'm just surprised.~
@11   = ~Eh... I'm getting tired of this... shamanic manner of speaking. Just apparitions and visions ALL THE TIME.~
@12   = ~Pleased to meet you, Will.~
@13   = ~It's fine. That's just the name my parents gave me. They would call me "Will-o'-wisp" as I loved stories about those. And zombies, of course. I was *that* kind of kid, I loved ghost stories. Ironic, isn't it? Now I'm a ghost myself and I "live" here with an actual wisp.~
@14   = ~Heh, life can be ironic and sometimes I think it especially enjoys playing on words associated with death.~
@15   = ~Ironic indeed, but a fine play it would make! Believe me or not, but 'tis a story hundreds would love to hear.~
@16   = ~Would you mind telling me how you became a ghost?~
@17   = ~Why are you here, spirit?~
@18   = ~Well, I suppose it could be worse.~
@19   = ~If you're so tired with the way we talk, why are you even here? You are the one who shapes your life and death, adventurer.~
@20   = ~You'd better tell me why you're here, ghost.~
@21   = ~Don't teach me about life, creature. Tell me why you're here.~
@22   = ~Fine, I'm here because I wanted to be. At least there're no demons here. It could be worse.~
@23   = ~I'm pleased to meet you, too, adventurer. I haven't met anyone for years.~
@24   = ~How did you become a ghost, Will?~
@25   = ~How come you're here? Shouldn't you be... resting, if you know what I mean?~
@26   = ~Consider yourself lucky, then. It could be worse. You could wake up and see and hear nothing.~
@27   = ~Worse? Well, I'm not the luckiest men of all, I'm afraid. Let me tell you a story. My story.~
@28   = ~I was once but a simple man with a love of stories. Stories about wisps, spirits, hags, sometimes skeletons or long forgotten rituals. Later in life I realized that such things went further than tale, and I was right. I studied life and death; spirits and powers. Though I was a weak man in life, I decided to leave my home behind. I visited a couple of places - ruins, forests, stone circles, before I learned of the Visionhive. I wandered through the forest, but one night--~
@29   = ~Well, I got bitten by a deathstrider snake, a vicious creature. I took care of the wound, cleaned it, drank brews to strengthen my body and continued my journey. But I still died. I fell onto the ground one hundred feet from the Visionhive I wished so desperately to see. But its protector found me, and buried me close enough so a part of me could remain here and support the grotto.~
@30   = ~So that shaman decided your fate for you, if I understood your story well.~
@31   = ~Well, I think you're lucky. Maybe you died, but you were found and buried. What's more, this Visionhive preserved your powers and your sanity. Many people die alone in darkness, but you were buried just next to the place you wanted to see.~
@32   = ~...and adter death you decided to bore people to death with your stories, right? Is this some sort of revange?~
@33   = ~Yes, but he had to do something, didn't he? Even if I could have spoken, I'm not sure what I would have tell him.~
@34   = ~Yes, you're right.~
@35   = ~You looked like someone who would enjoy such a torment.~
@36   = ~Nevertheless, I suppose you're not here to hear stories told by someone who has already passed away. What do you need from me?~
@37   = ~Do you want to join my group? I'm in need of someone with your skills.~
@38   = ~I'm the new protector of Visionhive. I thought I would talk to you, as I assume you're part of this place.~
@39   = ~I was wondering if you're fine.~
@40   = ~Nothing right now. I'll be leaving.~
@41   = ~As long as you can respect the spirits and death itself, yes, I will join. But I can't leave these woods until I visit one certain place. I literally can't, so don't even try negotiating. It will only take a minute to reach and then we can move on.~
@42   = ~I'll mark the place on your map. Come.~
@43   = ~Before we go, I must tell you that my primary mission is to find my friend Imoen. She was taken from me, and I believe it was all planned by an mad mage. I won't stop until I free her. She's like a sister to me, so I can't let her die.~
@44   = ~Good. But you should know something: I'm trying to find Jon Irenicus, an insane wizard who did experiments on me. He won't get away with what he's done. I want him to feel pain - just as I did when he locked me in his dungeons and did these horrible things to me.~
@45   = ~Fine. Let's go.~
@46   = ~Yes. This is my home and I feel bound within it, master shaman. No, more accurately... I *am* bound with the Visionhive.~
@47   = ~So, are you feeling fine? Do you need anything?~
@48   = ~I understand. Well, I think I'll be leaving.~
@49   = ~I'm as fine as anyone can feel years after death. I felt different since the restoration of the Visionhive, but... I also feel stronger, more stable.~
@50   = ~Would you like to join me in my adventures, Will?~
@51   = ~I'm the new protector of Visionhive, so I thought I would talk to you. After all, you're part of this place.~
@52   = ~Good. Well, now I'll be leaving.~
@53   = ~I understand. We should find this Imoen then, but remember - we must first visit the place I spoke of. It's near the grotto. Come, let's go.~
@54   = ~He tortured you? I can help you. No one would want you to return as a vengeful ghost if you can't put your matter at rest. But first... the place I spoke of. It's very close, near the grotto. Come.~
@55   = ~Fine. Let's go to the the place I mentioned. It's near the grotto.~
@56   = ~As you wish.~
@57   = ~You return. How can I help you?~
@58   = ~Could you tell me how you became a spectre?~
@59   = ~I hope you weren't too worried about me.~
@60   = ~Are you alright? Have you fully recovered?~
@61   = ~I figured you'd be alright. Ready to join me again?~
@62   = ~I just wanted to make sure you made it back safely. Wait for me here.~
@63   = ~I'm fine. It's a strange feeling to suddenly wake up here again... but I'm back to normal now. Should I rejoin you?~
@64   = ~Yes, join the group.~
@65   = ~I don't need you now, so... no.~
@66   = ~Ready as I can be. Let's go.~
@67   = ~Alright.~
@68   = ~*sigh* You know, <CHARNAME>, I can't say I enjoy being killed over and over again, even if I can come back... maybe it's too much to ask, but could you stop leading so... recklessly?~
@69   = ~I'm sorry, Will. I'll try to do a better job from now on.~
@70   = ~Better you than someone who's actually alive.~
@71   = ~Oh, quit whining and get back in the group again.~
@72   = ~Don't worry. You'll be staying here for now, away from danger.~
@73   = ~Good. I hope so. I can't believe I'm saying this, but I think I've grown sick of dying.~
@74   = ~I don't mind coming back here if it means I saved someone, but I can't believe I'd have to do this so many times.~
@75   = ~So, should I rejoin you? Hopefully I'll stay for longer this time.~
@76   = ~You returned for me?~
@77   = ~So you don't need me anymore? Do you want me to leave?~
@78   = ~Yes. I'm sorry. But perhaps one day we'll meet again.~
@79   = ~No, it's a mistake. You should stay.~
@80   = ~I understand. I'll return to my grave. You may meet me there.~
@81   = ~I'm glad.~

@18000   = ~Will has returned to his grave.~